window.INTERVIEW_EXECUTION["swift-36"] = {"timeBudget": {"navigation": 4, "solution": 9, "coding": 26, "verification": 6}, "navigation": {"say": "This is a two-sequence walk rather than a converging one, and the thing worth a minute up front is the window model: whether the ends are inclusive, and what the upstream roster actually guarantees about ordering. Both change the code, and neither is visible from the signatures.", "questions": ["Are the windows half-open, so a window ending where another begins shares nothing?", "Is each resource's list guaranteed sorted and internally non-overlapping, or do I have to earn that?", "Does the caller need to know which roster rows produced an overlap, or only the spans?", "For the gaps, is the horizon given, or should it be inferred from the windows themselves?"], "assumptions": ["Windows are half-open and measured in minutes since a planning epoch, so a shared instant is not availability.", "Each list arrives sorted by start and internally disjoint, which is what the rostering system guarantees, and a list that is not is a fault rather than something to sort here.", "Overlaps carry the contributing index in each list, because a dispatcher has to go back to the roster row.", "The gap query is asked of an already-coalesced list, which is why the coalescing ships alongside it."], "edgeCases": ["one or both lists empty, where there is nothing to intersect and the union is the other list", "windows that touch exactly, which share nothing but whose union is continuous", "one long window containing several short ones, where every overlap comes from the same left index", "a horizon that starts before or ends after every window, where the gaps run to the edges"], "restate": "Given per-resource availability windows, each list sorted and internally disjoint, report the spans two resources share, the spans a whole roster shares, the coalesced union of two lists, and what a coalesced list leaves uncovered inside a horizon."}, "solution": {"pitch": "One cursor per list, three times. This is the corner of the two-pointer family where the invariant has to be restated, because there is no band closing in and no write cursor lagging a read cursor: the guarantee is that everything already walked past has been fully accounted for. The move rule follows from that directly. Advance the list whose current window ends first, because that window can have no further intersections while the other one still might. The k-resource question is then a fold rather than a new algorithm, because the intersection of two sorted disjoint lists is itself sorted and disjoint.", "dataStructures": ["Two integer cursors and the output array. Nothing that grows with the input except the answer.", "For the union, the result array doubles as the working state: the span being built is always its last element.", "For the gaps, one integer cursor along the horizon, which is the whole state that query needs.", "No sorting anywhere, because the precondition supplies it, and the code checks the precondition rather than re-establishing it."], "types": ["A window is a struct with a duration property rather than a bare pair, because the duration identity in the third part reads much better against a name.", "It is Comparable so the union's merge step is a comparison rather than a tuple of conditionals.", "An overlap is its own type carrying both contributing indices, because a dispatcher needs the roster rows and not only the span.", "The failures are a typed enum, with the list ones naming the offending index, since the caller's next move is to go and look at that row."], "composition": "The k-resource question is a literal fold over the pairwise one, which is the composition the design is built around. The third part does not call the first, and I would rather be honest about that than manufacture a call: what ties them together is an identity the suite can assert on every input, that both lists together cover exactly the union plus the intersection. That is stronger than a call would be, because it can be red where a call would be green. An implementation that emits zero-length overlaps, or one that leaves a touching pair unmerged, breaks it immediately.", "tradeoff": "Carrying the contributing indices on every overlap costs two words per result and means the type cannot be a bare window. I want it, because the caller's next question is always which roster row to look at, and recovering that afterwards means a second search. If the spans alone were ever enough, projecting them out is one map.", "trap": "Two, and the first is the one that gets written by reflex: compare ends, not starts. Comparing starts still terminates and still produces a plausible-looking subset of the answer, which is exactly why it survives a quick read. The second is the empty intersection: with half-open windows the overlap is real only when its low bound is strictly below its high bound, and emitting a zero-length span there poisons every count downstream."}, "coding": {"order": ["Write the precondition check first. All four entry points need it and it is six lines.", "Write the intersection next, stating the advance rule out loud before writing the conditional that follows from it.", "Check it immediately against a fixture where one long window spans two short ones, which is where comparing starts goes visibly wrong.", "Write the fold, saying why the closure property makes it legal rather than needing a k-cursor sweep.", "Write the union, and decide the touching case deliberately rather than by whichever comparison operator comes out of your fingers.", "Write the gaps last as a cursor along the horizon, and remember the tail after the final window."], "commentary": ["There is no converging band here, so I restate the invariant: after each step, every answer involving a window I have already passed has been emitted.", "That is what tells me which cursor moves. The window that ends first cannot intersect anything else, because everything left in the other list starts at or after a window that ends later than it does.", "So I compare ends. Comparing starts is the version that terminates and quietly returns a subset, which is the sort of bug that survives a code review.", "The windows are half-open, so I only emit when the low bound is strictly below the high bound. A zero-length overlap is not an overlap.", "For k resources, the previous part's output is sorted and disjoint just like its input, so folding is legal and a k-cursor sweep would be the same algorithm written a second time.", "I validate every list before folding, because once the running intersection empties, a validation carried by the fold would never look at what is left.", "For the union I coalesce on touch as well as on overlap: two windows meeting at an instant describe one continuous span of availability.", "The gaps assume a coalesced list, which is why the coalescing lives in the same part rather than being assumed by it."], "checkIn": "Before writing the union I would say: I am going to merge two windows that merely touch into one span, on the grounds that availability meeting at an instant is continuous rather than two options. If your dispatcher would rather see the boundary, that is a one-character change and now is the moment to make it."}, "verification": {"walkthroughs": ["Walk a driver free from eight to twelve and fourteen to eighteen against a vehicle free from nine to ten, eleven to sixteen and seventeen to twenty: the shared spans are nine to ten, eleven to twelve, fourteen to sixteen, and seventeen to eighteen.", "Walk the same pair with the move rule comparing starts and watch eleven to twelve disappear while the sweep still finishes cleanly.", "Add a dock free only from nine to fifteen and fold: the three-way answer shortens the third span to fourteen to fifteen and drops the fourth entirely.", "Check the identity on that pair by hand: eight minutes of driver plus nine of vehicle is seventeen, and their union of twelve plus their intersection of five is seventeen as well.", "Take the union of two windows that touch at an instant and confirm one span comes back rather than two."], "tests": "Run the matcher's suite and read the three parts in order. The worked roster in the first suite is the fixture that catches comparing starts, and the touching pair is what catches emitting zero-length overlaps. The third suite asserts the duration identity on every pair of fixtures, which is the cross-part check this problem can honestly make, and it also asserts that the gaps and the union together partition the horizon exactly.", "complexity": "Every part is linear in the total number of windows it is handed, with constant extra state beyond the output. The fold over k resources is linear in the sum of the list lengths, since the running intersection never grows larger than the shorter input. The temptation to reach for a sort or an interval tree is worth naming and declining: the precondition already supplies the order, and checking it costs one pass.", "moreTime": "Weighted resources, where a span is only useful if it is at least some minimum length, which turns the intersection into a filter and changes what the fold can discard early. Recurring weekly availability, where windows wrap a period and the sweep needs a normalising pass first. And a streaming roster, where lists arrive incrementally and the fold has to keep its running intersection across arrivals."}, "code": "public struct Window: Equatable, Comparable, Sendable {\n    public let start: Int\n    public let end: Int\n\n    public init(_ start: Int, _ end: Int) {\n        self.start = start\n        self.end = end\n    }\n\n    /// Windows are half-open, so a window that ends where another begins\n    /// contributes nothing to the overlap between them.\n    public var duration: Int { end - start }\n\n    public static func < (lhs: Window, rhs: Window) -> Bool {\n        lhs.start == rhs.start ? lhs.end < rhs.end : lhs.start < rhs.start\n    }\n}\n\npublic struct Overlap: Equatable, Sendable {\n    public let window: Window\n    public let leftIndex: Int\n    public let rightIndex: Int\n\n    public init(window: Window, leftIndex: Int, rightIndex: Int) {\n        self.window = window\n        self.leftIndex = leftIndex\n        self.rightIndex = rightIndex\n    }\n}\n\npublic enum WindowError: Error, Equatable, Sendable {\n    case emptyOrInvertedWindow(start: Int, end: Int)\n    case unsortedInput(index: Int)\n    case overlappingInput(index: Int)\n    case noResources\n    case notImplemented\n}\n\npublic struct DispatchMatcher: Sendable {\n    public init() {}\n\n    // MARK: Part 1 - Intersect two sorted window lists\n\n    /// Every span during which both resources are simultaneously free.\n    ///\n    /// One cursor per list. There is no converging band here and no lagging\n    /// write cursor: the two cursors walk two different sequences, so the\n    /// invariant has to be restated as \"every intersection involving a window\n    /// already passed has been emitted\".\n    ///\n    /// The move rule follows from that restatement. The window that ends first\n    /// can have no further intersections, because every remaining window in the\n    /// other list starts at or after the window the other cursor stands on,\n    /// which itself ends later. So the list whose current window ends first is\n    /// the one that advances, and nothing is stepped over.\n    public func overlaps(_ left: [Window], _ right: [Window]) throws(WindowError) -> [Overlap] {\n        try validate(left)\n        try validate(right)\n\n        var overlaps: [Overlap] = []\n        var i = 0\n        var j = 0\n\n        while i < left.count, j < right.count {\n            let lo = max(left[i].start, right[j].start)\n            let hi = min(left[i].end, right[j].end)\n\n            // Half-open windows only genuinely overlap when lo is strictly\n            // below hi. Emitting lo == hi manufactures zero-length overlaps\n            // that inflate every downstream count.\n            if lo < hi {\n                overlaps.append(Overlap(window: Window(lo, hi), leftIndex: i, rightIndex: j))\n            }\n\n            // Ends, not starts. Comparing starts still terminates, which is\n            // what makes it such a comfortable bug.\n            if left[i].end < right[j].end {\n                i += 1\n            } else {\n                j += 1\n            }\n        }\n\n        return overlaps\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 2 - Common availability across k resources\n\n    /// The spans during which every listed resource is simultaneously free.\n    ///\n    /// This folds the pairwise intersection across the resources rather than\n    /// generalising the sweep to k cursors: the intersection of sorted disjoint\n    /// lists is itself sorted and disjoint, which is exactly what makes the\n    /// fold legal and what makes a k-cursor rewrite unnecessary.\n    public func commonAvailability(of lists: [[Window]]) throws(WindowError) -> [Window] {\n        guard let first = lists.first else { throw .noResources }\n\n        // Every roster is checked before the fold starts. A malformed list is a\n        // fault whether or not the fold would have reached it: once the running\n        // intersection empties, nothing later can put a span back, and a\n        // validation that rode along with the fold would fall silent exactly\n        // then.\n        for list in lists {\n            try validate(list)\n        }\n\n        var running = first\n        for list in lists.dropFirst() {\n            running = try overlaps(running, list).map(\\.window)\n        }\n\n        return running\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Part 3 - Coalesced union and its gaps\n\n    /// Every span during which at least one of the two resources is free, with\n    /// touching and overlapping spans coalesced into one.\n    ///\n    /// The same two cursors, now taking whichever window starts first instead\n    /// of intersecting them. Coalescing on touch as well as on overlap is what\n    /// makes the result a genuine union: two windows that meet at an instant\n    /// describe one continuous span of availability.\n    public func coalescedUnion(_ left: [Window], _ right: [Window]) throws(WindowError) -> [Window] {\n        try validate(left)\n        try validate(right)\n\n        var merged: [Window] = []\n        var i = 0\n        var j = 0\n\n        while i < left.count || j < right.count {\n            let next: Window\n            if j == right.count || (i < left.count && left[i] < right[j]) {\n                next = left[i]\n                i += 1\n            } else {\n                next = right[j]\n                j += 1\n            }\n\n            if let last = merged.last, next.start <= last.end {\n                merged[merged.count - 1] = Window(last.start, max(last.end, next.end))\n            } else {\n                merged.append(next)\n            }\n        }\n\n        return merged\n    }\n\n    /// The spans inside the horizon that the given windows do not cover.\n    ///\n    /// The windows are expected to be coalesced already, which is why the\n    /// coalescing lives in this part rather than being assumed by it: gaps of a\n    /// list that still holds two windows describing one span are not defined.\n    public func gaps(in windows: [Window], within horizon: Window) throws(WindowError) -> [Window] {\n        try validate(windows)\n        guard horizon.end > horizon.start else {\n            throw .emptyOrInvertedWindow(start: horizon.start, end: horizon.end)\n        }\n\n        var gaps: [Window] = []\n        var cursor = horizon.start\n\n        for window in windows {\n            let start = max(window.start, horizon.start)\n            let end = min(window.end, horizon.end)\n            guard start < end else { continue }\n\n            if start > cursor {\n                gaps.append(Window(cursor, start))\n            }\n            cursor = max(cursor, end)\n        }\n\n        if cursor < horizon.end {\n            gaps.append(Window(cursor, horizon.end))\n        }\n\n        return gaps\n    }\n\n    // MARK: Shared validation\n\n    /// The precondition every part rests on: each window is a real span, and\n    /// the list is sorted and internally disjoint. All three parts read the\n    /// list assuming this, so all three check it.\n    private func validate(_ windows: [Window]) throws(WindowError) {\n        for (index, window) in windows.enumerated() {\n            guard window.end > window.start else {\n                throw .emptyOrInvertedWindow(start: window.start, end: window.end)\n            }\n            guard index > 0 else { continue }\n            let previous = windows[index - 1]\n            guard window.start >= previous.start else { throw .unsortedInput(index: index) }\n            guard window.start >= previous.end else { throw .overlappingInput(index: index) }\n        }\n    }\n}\n"};
